{
  "id": "Compound::DB00445 - Disease::DOID:1781",
  "verbalization": "Epirubicin is implicated in the development of thyroid cancer through a chain of biological interactions involving doxorubicin, an anthracycline antineoplastic antibiotic. The evidence suggests that Epirubicin may contribute to the pathogenesis of thyroid cancer by causing conditions such as urethral obstruction, myelodysplasia, and bladder constriction, all of which can lead to complications that may influence cancer progression. These effects are mediated through a common ancestor, the primary alpha-hydroxy ketone, and the anthracycline class of drugs. Doxorubicin, in turn, is known to treat thyroid cancer, indicating a potential therapeutic relationship. The integration of these pathways suggests that Epirubicin may indirectly contribute to the development of thyroid cancer by influencing cellular processes that are linked to the disease. The strength of the evidence is consistent across multiple paths, but the exact mechanism by which Epirubicin contributes to thyroid cancer remains to be fully elucidated.",
